As is evident from the table, these business management processes were even more erratically completed than the legal and governance processes. A substantial proportion of CFMGs did not have workplans, budgets or a business plan. More surprising, many did not have any recognised user groups, which is critical to managing forest resources.

## Governance index

To summarise CFMG governance performance, we constructed a governance index (0–10) based on the presence or absence of ten selected features reported by CFMG executives: receipt book, bank account, financial reporting, annual reporting, issuing permits, HFOs appointed, workplan, budget, business plan, and term limits. We then tested whether two aspects of external support — the frequency of district forest office staff visits and the presence of a partner organisation — were associated with higher governance scores (@fig-gov_index; Appendix III).


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Distribution of CFMG governance index scores (0–10). Each point represents one CFMG. The index counts the number of ten governance features reported as present by the CFMG executive FGD.](CFMG-Governance_files/figure-pdf/fig-gov_index-1.pdf){#fig-gov_index}
:::
:::






